=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** router4's two uplink interfaces — eth4 (link_r4_r5, toward router5) and eth5 (link_r4_r3, toward router3) — are both operationally DOWN while the peer ends of both links (router5.eth5 and router3.eth5) are UP and healthy.

**Mechanism:** The topology runs RIP (`ripd`) as its inter-site routing protocol. Because router4's uplinks are DOWN, `ripd` on router4 cannot form any RIP sessions; consequently, router4 never advertises its directly-connected prefix `192.168.200.0/24` (Location C) into the RIP domain. No other router — including router3, router2, router0, or router6 — has a route to 192.168.200.x in its forwarding table. Packets sourced from Location A (172.16.10.x) or Location D (172.18.51.x) destined for Location C are dropped with "Destination Host Unreachable" or silently discarded. Hosts within Location C (pc11–pc14) can still reach each other because they share the same L2 segment and never need to traverse router4's uplinks.

**Localization:** router4, interfaces eth4 and eth5 (both state DOWN, IPs 192.168.15.2 and 172.25.1.1).

**Root cause:** Both uplink interfaces on router4 are in DOWN state, completely isolating it from the RIP routing domain and preventing the 192.168.200.0/24 prefix from being advertised — Location C is entirely unreachable from all other sites. Router3 is not at fault; its side of both links is fully UP.

**Fix:** Bring up the two interfaces on router4: `ip link set eth4 up && ip link set eth5 up`. Once UP, `ripd` will form sessions with router3 and router5, advertise 192.168.200.0/24, and full inter-site reachability will be restored. Do **not** replace router3.
=== END ===